\id 1TI - The Text-Critical English New Testament
\h THE FIRST LETTER OF PAUL TO TIMOTHY
\toc1 The First Letter of Paul to Timothy
\toc2 1 Timothy
\toc3 1 Tim.
\mt2 THE FIRST LETTER OF PAUL TO
\mt1 TIMOTHY
\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\m
\v 1 Paul, an apostle of \f + \fr 1:1 \fq Jesus Christ \ft ¦ Christ Jesus CT\f*Jesus Christ according to the command of God our Savior and \f + \fr 1:1 \fq the Lord Jesus Christ \ft ¦ Christ Jesus CT\f*the Lord Jesus Christ our hope,
\v 2 to Timothy, my true child in the faith: Grace, mercy, and peace from God \f + \fr 1:2 \fq our \ft ¦ the CT\f*our Father and \f + \fr 1:2 \fq Christ Jesus \ft ¦ Jesus Christ SCR\f*Christ Jesus our Lord.
\s1 Warning Against False Teachers
\m
\v 3 Just as I urged yoʋ when I was going to Macedonia, remain in Ephesus so that yoʋ may charge certain people not to teach different doctrines
\v 4 or to occupy themselves with myths and endless genealogies, which promote disputes rather than the \f + \fr 1:4 \fq plan of God that operates by \ft ¦ edification of God, which is in ELZ SCR\f*plan of God that operates by faith.
\v 5 The goal of our instruction is love from a pure heart, from a good conscience, and from sincere faith.
\v 6 Some have strayed from these and have turned aside to meaningless talk,
\v 7 desiring to be teachers of the law, although they do not understand what they are talking about or what they so confidently affirm.
\p
\v 8 Now we know that the law is good, if one uses it lawfully.
\v 9 We know that the law is not laid down for the righteous, but for the lawless and rebellious, for the ungodly and sinners, for the unholy and profane, for those who kill their father or mother, for murderers,
\v 10 fornicators, homosexuals, slave traders, liars, perjurers, and whatever else is opposed to the sound teaching
\v 11 that conforms to the gospel of the glory of the blessed God, which has been entrusted to me.
\s1 Paul's Testimony of God's Mercy
\m
\v 12 I give thanks to Christ Jesus our Lord, who has given me strength, because he considered me to be faithful and appointed me to serve him,
\v 13 even though I was formerly a blasphemer, a persecutor, and an insolent man. But I received mercy because I had acted ignorantly in unbelief,
\v 14 and the grace of our Lord abounded to me greatly with the faith and love that are in Christ Jesus.
\v 15 This saying is trustworthy and deserving of full acceptance: Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, of whom I am the foremost.
\v 16 But I received mercy for this reason, that in me, as the foremost sinner, \f + \fr 1:16 \fq Jesus Christ \ft ¦ Christ Jesus NA SBL WH\f*Jesus Christ might show complete patience, making me an example for those who would come to believe in him for eternal life.
\v 17 Now to the King of the ages, immortal, invisible, the only \f + \fr 1:17 \fq wise \ft 96.6% ¦ — CT 1.8%\f*wise God, be honor and glory forever and ever. Amen.
\p
\v 18 This charge I entrust to yoʋ, Timothy, my child, according to the prophecies once made about yoʋ, so that by them yoʋ may fight the good fight,
\v 19 having faith and a good conscience. By rejecting this, some have shipwrecked their faith.
\v 20 Among them are Hymenaeus and Alexander, whom I have handed over to Satan so that they may learn not to blaspheme.
\c 2
\s1 Instruction About Prayer
\m
\v 1 I urge then, first of all, that supplications, prayers, intercessions, and thanksgivings be made for all people,
\v 2 for kings and all who are in authority, so that we may lead quiet and peaceful lives in all godliness and dignity.
\v 3 \f + \fr 2:3 \fq For this \ft ¦ This CT\f*For this is good and acceptable before God our Savior,
\v 4 who wants all people to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth.
\v 5 For there is one God and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus,
\v 6 who gave himself as a ransom for all, a testimony given at the proper time.
\v 7 For this testimony I was appointed to be a preacher, an apostle, and a teacher of the Gentiles in faith and truth. (I am speaking the truth \f + \fr 2:7 \fq in Christ \ft ¦ — CT\f*in Christ; I am not lying.)
\p
\v 8 Therefore I want the men in every place to pray, lifting up holy hands without anger or \f + \fr 2:8 \fq argument \ft ¦ arguments WH\f*argument.
\v 9 Likewise, I want the women to adorn themselves in respectable apparel, with modesty and discretion, not with elaborately braided \f + \fr 2:9 \fq hair, gold, pearls, \ft ¦ hair and gold, or pearls CT\f*hair, gold, pearls, or expensive clothing,
\v 10 but with good works, which is proper for women who profess godly reverence.
\v 11 A woman should learn quietly with full submission.
\v 12 I do not permit a woman to teach or to have authority over a man; rather, she is to remain quiet.
\v 13 For Adam was formed first, then Eve.
\v 14 And Adam was not the one who was deceived; it was the woman who was deceived and fell into transgression.
\v 15 Nevertheless, women will be saved through childbearing, if they continue in faith, love, and holiness, with self-control.
\c 3
\s1 Qualifications for Overseers
\m
\v 1 This saying is trustworthy: If anyone aspires to the office of overseer, he desires a good work.
\v 2 Therefore the overseer must be above reproach, the husband of one wife, sober-minded, sensible, respectable, hospitable, able to teach,
\v 3 not given to wine, not violent, \f + \fr 3:3 \fq not greedy for sordid gain, \ft 69.5% ¦ — CT 29.5%\f*not greedy for sordid gain, but gentle, not contentious, and not a lover of money.
\v 4 He must lead his own household well, keeping his children in submission with all dignity.
\v 5 (For if a man does not know how to lead his own household, how will he take care of God's church?)
\v 6 He must not be a new convert, or he may become puffed up and fall into the condemnation of the devil.
\v 7 He must also have a good reputation among outsiders, so that he does not fall into the reproach and snare of the devil.
\s1 Qualifications for Deacons
\m
\v 8 Deacons likewise must be dignified, not double-tongued, not given to much wine, and not greedy for sordid gain.
\v 9 They must hold the mystery of the faith with a clear conscience.
\v 10 They must first be tested; if they are above reproach, let them serve as deacons.
\v 11 Their wives likewise must be dignified, not slanderers, but sober-minded and faithful in all things.
\v 12 A deacon must be the husband of one wife and must lead his children and his own household well.
\v 13 For those who have served well as deacons obtain a good standing for themselves along with great boldness in the faith that is in Christ Jesus.
\s1 The Mystery of Godliness
\m
\v 14 I am writing these things to yoʋ, hoping to come to yoʋ soon.
\v 15 But if I delay, I am writing so that yoʋ may know how one ought to conduct himself in the household of God, which is the church of the living God, a pillar and foundation of the truth.
\v 16 Beyond all question, great is the mystery of godliness:
\b
\q1 \f + \fr 3:16 \fq God \ft ¦ He CT\f*God was revealed in the flesh,
\q1 vindicated by the Spirit,
\q1 seen by angels,
\q1 preached among nations,
\q1 believed on in the world,
\q1 and taken up in glory.
\c 4
\s1 Some Will Fall Away from the Faith
\m
\v 1 Now the Spirit expressly says that in latter times some will fall away from the faith and occupy themselves with deceitful spirits and the teachings of demons,
\v 2 being influenced by the hypocrisy of liars who are seared in their own consciences.
\v 3 They forbid marriage and demand abstinence from foods that God created to be received with thanksgiving by those who believe and know the truth.
\v 4 For everything created by God is good, and nothing is to be rejected when it is received with thanksgiving,
\v 5 since it is sanctified through the word of God and prayer.
\s1 A Good Servant of Jesus Christ
\m
\v 6 If yoʋ point these things out to the brothers, yoʋ will be a good servant of \f + \fr 4:6 \fq Jesus Christ \ft ¦ Christ Jesus CT\f*Jesus Christ, nourished by the words of the faith and the sound doctrine that yoʋ have closely followed.
\v 7 Have nothing to do with profane myths and old wives' tales. Rather train yoʋrself for godliness.
\v 8 For bodily training is beneficial to a certain extent, but godliness is beneficial in every way, since it holds \f + \fr 4:8 \fq promises \ft ¦ promise CT HF RP TR\f*promises for both the present life and the life to come.
\v 9 This saying is trustworthy and deserving of full acceptance.
\v 10 For this is why we labor and \f + \fr 4:10 \fq suffer reproach \ft ¦ strive NA TH WH\f*suffer reproach, because we have put our hope in the living God, who is the Savior of all people, and especially of those who believe.
\p
\v 11 Command and teach these things.
\v 12 Let no one despise yoʋ because yoʋ are young, but set an example for the believers in yoʋr speech, conduct, love, \f + \fr 4:12 \fq spirit, \ft 90.4% ¦ — CT 9%\f*spirit, faith, and purity.
\v 13 Until I come, give attention to the public reading of Scripture, to exhortation, and to teaching.
\v 14 Do not neglect the gift that is in yoʋ, which was given to yoʋ through prophecy when the council of elders laid their hands on yoʋ.
\v 15 Attend to these things and immerse yourself in them, so that yoʋr progress may be evident to all.
\v 16 Watch yoʋr life and doctrine closely. Persevere in these things, for as yoʋ do this, yoʋ will save both yoʋrself and those who listen to yoʋ.
\c 5
\s1 Instructions for the Church
\m
\v 1 Do not rebuke an older man, but appeal to him as you would to a father. Speak to younger men as brothers,
\v 2 older women as mothers, and younger women as sisters, in all purity.
\p
\v 3 Honor widows who are truly widows.
\v 4 But if any widow has children or grandchildren, they should first learn to show godliness toward their own household and to make some repayment to their parents, for this is \f + \fr 5:4 \fq acceptable \ft ¦ good and acceptable ANT TR\f*acceptable in the sight of God.
\v 5 Now a woman who is truly a widow and left all alone has put her hope in God and continues night and day in her supplications and prayers.
\v 6 But she who lives in self-indulgence is dead even while she lives.
\v 7 Give these commands as well, so that no one will be open to blame.
\v 8 But if anyone does not provide for his own relatives, and especially for those of his own household, he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever.
\p
\v 9 A widow may be put on the list to receive support if she is at least sixty years old, has been the wife of one husband,
\v 10 and has a reputation for good works, that is, if she has brought up children, shown hospitality, washed the feet of the saints, helped the afflicted, and devoted herself to every good work.
\v 11 But reject younger widows, for when their sensual desires draw them away from Christ, they wish to marry,
\v 12 thus bringing judgment upon themselves because they have broken their previous pledge.
\v 13 At the same time, they learn to be idle, wandering about from house to house. And they are not only idle, but also gossips and busybodies, saying things they should not say.
\v 14 So I advise the younger widows to marry, bear children, and manage their households, so as to give the adversary no opportunity to revile us.
\v 15 For some have already turned away to follow Satan.
\v 16 If any believing \f + \fr 5:16 \fq man or woman … they \ft 95.1% ¦ woman … she CT 1.9%\f*man or woman has relatives who are widows, they must help them; the church should not be burdened, so that it may help those who are truly widows.
\p
\v 17 Elders who lead well should be considered worthy of double honor, especially those who labor in preaching and teaching.
\v 18 For the Scripture says, “Yoʋ shall not muzzle an ox while it is threshing,” and, \wj “The worker is worthy of his wages.”\wj*
\v 19 Do not accept an accusation against an elder unless it is confirmed by two or three witnesses.
\v 20 As for those who sin, reprove them in the presence of all, so that others will be afraid to follow their example.
\v 21 I solemnly charge yoʋ before God and \f + \fr 5:21 \fq the Lord Jesus Christ \ft ¦ Christ Jesus CT\f*the Lord Jesus Christ and the chosen angels to observe these things without partiality, doing nothing out of favoritism.
\v 22 Do not lay hands on anyone hastily, and do not take part in the sins of others; keep yoʋrself pure.
\v 23 (No longer drink only water, but use a little wine for the sake of yoʋr stomach and yoʋr frequent illnesses.)
\v 24 The sins of some are obvious, going before them to judgment, but the sins of others trail along behind.
\v 25 In the same way, good works are obvious, and even those that are not obvious cannot remain hidden.
\c 6
\m
\v 1 All who are under the yoke of slavery must regard their own masters as worthy of all honor, so that no one will revile God's name or his doctrine.
\v 2 Slaves who have believing masters must not despise them, for they are brothers; rather they must serve them, because those who benefit from their good service are faithful and beloved.
\s1 False Teachers and the Love of Money
\m Teach and encourage these things.
\v 3 If anyone teaches a different doctrine and does not agree with the sound words of our Lord Jesus Christ and the teaching that is in accordance with godliness,
\v 4 he is puffed up and understands nothing. Moreover, he has an unhealthy desire for controversies and quarrels about words, from which come envy, strife, slanderous words, evil suspicions,
\v 5 and constant disagreement among people who are depraved in mind and deprived of the truth, supposing that godliness is a means of gain. \f + \fr 6:5 \fq Keep away from such people. \ft 92.5% ¦ — CT 2.9%\f*Keep away from such people.
\v 6 But godliness with contentment is great gain.
\v 7 For we brought nothing into the world, and \f + \fr 6:7 \fq it is clear that \ft ¦ — CT\f*it is clear that we cannot bring anything out either;
\v 8 but if we have food and clothing, we will be content with that.
\v 9 But those who wish to be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many senseless and harmful desires that sink people into ruin and destruction.
\v 10 For the love of money is the root of all kinds of evil, and in their eagerness to become rich some have wandered away from the faith, piercing themselves with many sorrows.
\s1 Fight the Good Fight
\m
\v 11 But as for yoʋ, O man of God, flee from these things, and pursue righteousness, godliness, faithfulness, love, endurance, and gentleness.
\v 12 Fight the good fight of faith. Take hold of the eternal life to which yoʋ were called and concerning which yoʋ made the good confession in the presence of many witnesses.
\v 13 I charge yoʋ before God, who gives life to all things, and before Christ Jesus, who made the good confession in his testimony before Pontius Pilate,
\v 14 that yoʋ obey what has been commanded without fault or reproach until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ,
\v 15 which God will reveal in his own time. He is the blessed and only Sovereign, the King of kings and Lord of lords,
\v 16 who alone has immortality, dwelling in unapproachable light, whom no man has ever seen or is able to see. To him be honor and eternal power. Amen.
\p
\v 17 Command those who are rich in this present age not to be haughty or to put their hope in the uncertainty of riches, but in \f + \fr 6:17 \fq the living \ft 84.3% ¦ — CT 6.9%\f*the living God, who richly provides us with everything for our enjoyment.
\v 18 Command them to do good, to be rich in good works, and to be generous and willing to share,
\v 19 treasuring up for themselves a good foundation for the time to come, so that they may take hold of \f + \fr 6:19 \fq eternal \ft ¦ that which is truly CT\f*eternal life.
\p
\v 20 O Timothy, guard what has been entrusted to yoʋ and avoid the profane chatter and counterarguments of what is falsely called “knowledge.”
\v 21 By professing it, some have strayed from the faith.
\p Grace be with \f + \fr 6:21 \fq yoʋ \ft ¦ you CT\f*yoʋ.
\d \f + \fr 6:21 \fq Amen. \ft ¦ — CT\f*Amen.
